ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411056 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b19ad54e-7fbb-5207-bf8a-3ab93b802ddf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411058 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $M⇒¬N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17e8ba9f-418a-585c-9332-bdf5c1ee589a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411058 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nM⇒¬N≡¬M∨¬N\n$$\n\nUsing De Morgan's law, we reach\n\n$$\n¬M∨¬N≡¬(M∧N).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬(M∧N).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If you follow the recipe, the dish will be delicious.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "879f4dd5-148a-578f-a2fe-b0846abdf946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411063 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You follow the recipe.\n\n- $B:$ The dish will be delicious.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not follow the recipe.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not wear a seatbelt, or you'll be safe.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94ae2108-4770-5706-9cb7-823f98b8c863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411064 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not wear a seatbelt.\n\n- $B:$ You'll be safe.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You wear a seatbelt.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you wear a seatbelt, then you'll be safe.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you wear a seatbelt, you'll be safe.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(X⇒Y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb0a268b-ca46-5b91-9359-0e418e71bab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411066 #edb/ref 1074 "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(X⇒Y)≡¬(¬X∨Y)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬X∨Y)≡¬(¬X)∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∧¬Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186422176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1045 "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186422172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422157 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ad31faf4-6ba4-59e5-b6eb-0f068c9137a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422158 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧(Q∧R)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422158 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422158 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "173a22f9-03eb-5f52-84a2-64add79bb270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422159 #edb/ref 1080 "P∨(Q∨R)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422159 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422159 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2655898f-0ab3-55b9-b970-24883e6a1310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422160 #edb/ref 1080 "If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38c9c24e-3b55-56ca-86a7-b2ce01e8f281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422161 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧(Q∨¬R)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422161 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422161 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "666720f8-ee5e-5a0c-9eff-9b28566b29c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422162 #edb/ref 1080 "If you apologize, then she will forgive you." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422162 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422162 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "719cf9b3-0ac6-54a8-b955-880ab8843c40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422163 #edb/ref 1080 "If you apologize, then she won't forgive you." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422163 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422163 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8213100f-0dc7-5d4d-b8fd-6e029e273dc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422164 #edb/ref 1080 "If you don't apologize, then she will forgive you." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422164 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422164 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a2794c58-1f77-5658-9986-016411aec9fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422165 #edb/ref 1080 "Apologize, and she will forgive you." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422165 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422165 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a2993367-1981-5e54-b3ef-99b9be03f44e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422166 #edb/ref 1080 "P∧(Q∨R)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422166 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422166 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be278a98-7931-527b-9249-5fe023ad7c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422167 #edb/ref 1080 "¬P∧(Q∨R)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422167 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422167 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d2702799-977f-5026-b7cd-3d37fe168a4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422168 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e83d217a-41be-555a-a16a-343371ec8880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186422167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186422166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422169 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f19fca36-2b89-53ba-85fc-7765f2211e66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422170 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bdcd5e4-a0e7-512a-97bb-f847c06388f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422170 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(P∧Q)⇒(P∧R)≡¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(P∧Q))∨(P∧R)≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧R).\n$$\n\nFinally, by the distributive laws, we have\n\n$$\n(P∧Q)∨(P∧R)≡P∧(Q∨R).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $P∧(Q∨R).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422171 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186422168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422171 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422171 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422171 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ Either apologize, or she won't forgive you.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4482556-1279-5932-bd2e-cbe957830200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422171 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Apologize.\n\n- $B:$ She won't forgive you.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't apologize.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't apologize, then she won't forgive you.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422172 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186422174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422172 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12d4c7cd-cbe3-5cf6-98ac-5ef2b58306b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422172 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186378712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422173 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186422176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422173 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "992a12e5-4030-5d06-bfe4-99bff662d13d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422173 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422174 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186422175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422174 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4bf2a7e-915f-5aaa-9350-9113ac1d5a6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422174 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422175 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186422173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422175 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4e7f46a-d2bb-5a13-8fe3-d393ec767bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422175 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186422170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422176 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec1c4e29-ed0c-5082-8077-e13084a4137b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186422176 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186422171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428708 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428708 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 2 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1, 2]; waits [1, 1]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (2/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing cov